AP-60 / QA[8] — task_bug: tautological composition guard (LOW, breaks_ac: false)

WHERE
  tests/Feature/FrozenCorpusDeterminismTest.php:99-108
    test_the_composition_still_straddles_the_band_exit_threshold()
  app/Support/FrozenCorpusBuilder.php:45,47,137-142

WHAT ITS DOCBLOCK CLAIMS (line 97-98)
  "The composition thresholds the mutation matrix rests on. If the corpus ever drifts across one of
   them, a mutation demo stops firing for its stated reason — silently."

WHAT IT ACTUALLY ASSERTS
  $threshold = 1 - FrozenCorpusBuilder::TOKENIZER['band']['min']
                 / FrozenCorpusBuilder::TOKENIZER['chars_per_token'];      // class constant
  assertGreaterThan($threshold, $this->manifest['composition']['signature_char_share']);
  assertLessThan   ($threshold, $this->manifest['composition']['attachment_char_share']);

  ...and manifest.composition is WRITTEN FROM THE SAME CLASS CONSTANTS:
    FrozenCorpusBuilder.php:45   private const SIGNATURE_SHARE  = 0.32;
    FrozenCorpusBuilder.php:47   private const ATTACHMENT_SHARE = 0.114;
    FrozenCorpusBuilder.php:138  'signature_char_share'  => self::SIGNATURE_SHARE,
    FrozenCorpusBuilder.php:139  'attachment_char_share' => self::ATTACHMENT_SHARE,

  => it compares a DECLARED constant against a constant-derived threshold. It never measures the
     emitted .jsonl bytes, so it cannot detect the corpus drift its docblock names.

PROOF IT IS ALREADY BLIND (no mutation needed — the declaration has ALREADY gone stale)
  declared  manifest.composition.attachment_char_share = 0.1140  (11.40%)
  REALISED  (independent oracle, straight from the raw frozen bytes) = 0.11867 (11.87%)
  -> 0.47pp apart, test green. The number it purports to guard has already moved and it did not notice.

PROOF BY MUTATION (corpus bytes edited, manifest untouched, then git checkout -- restored)
  Truncated every thinking.signature to 35% of its length (realised signature share 32% -> ~13%,
  i.e. BELOW the 22.18% band-exit threshold the whole --no-signature demo rests on):

    test_the_composition_still_straddles_the_band_exit_threshold ... PASSED   <-- did not fire
    (a guard that stays green while the very quantity it names collapses is not a guard)

MITIGATION — the risk IS covered, by a sibling that measures
  tests/Feature/CalibrateFrozenCorpusTest.php:162 test_no_signature_fails_only_on_the_band()
  runs the real command against the real bytes and asserts assertFalse($r['band_pass']). On a
  faithful regeneration with SIGNATURE_SHARE below the threshold, --no-signature would yield
  band_pass=true and THAT test fails loudly. Likewise test_the_gate_passes_on_the_frozen_corpus()
  fails if the baseline k leaves the band.

  So this is NOT a coverage hole and NOT an AC failure — the shipped gate is correct today
  (independently verified: signature 32.00% > 22.18%, attachments 11.87% < 22.18%, ~10pp margin
  on BOTH sides — neither mutation demo is passing by luck). It is a test that cannot fail,
  carrying a docblock that says it can, in a repo whose signature failure mode is exactly that
  (AP-33 shipped a 16.8%-wrong bucket_system under a green suite; AP-47 QA caught a circular
  DB read-back).

SUGGESTED FIX (engineer, not QA)
  Make the guard MEASURE: derive signature_char_share / attachment_char_share from the emitted
  frozen bytes (the builder already sums $b['sig'] and $b['att'] in close(), lines 486-489 — write
  the REALISED values into manifest.composition instead of the design constants), then assert those
  against the threshold. That single change makes the test falsifiable and simultaneously fixes the
  stale 0.114 vs 0.1187 declaration.
